    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "Heres why that matters so much. Think about any country that needs oil. Japan, India, Germany, Kenya. To buy it, they first need dollars. Even when two countries that arent America trade oil with each other, the price is usually set in dollars. So they sell goods to America, borrow in dollars, or keep huge piles of dollars in reserve, just in case. Meanwhile, the oil sellers send their dollars back into American banks and American bonds. Money flows out of America to pay for oil, and flows right back in. Economists call it petrodollar recycling. And for America, its an incredible deal. Constant demand for its currency means it can borrow more cheaply, and run deficits that would sink almost any other country.",
